foreign  night in the capital of Peru protesters  clashed with police as they tried to  stop angry peruvians from reaching the  Parliament building  thousands marched through Lima demanding  the resignation of president  dinaboluarte  some threw rocks and bottles and  security forces responded with tear gas  several protesters and police were  injured today the people are rising up  not because we want to but because of  the conviction we have for a better Peru  president Dina palawate has to resign  an empty building in the city's historic  Center caught fire although the cost is  yet unknown the protesters are calling  for the resignation of the president  that the solution of Congress fresh  elections and a new constitution  God will help us we will win sooner than  later if she does not resign the people  will kick her out and we will die for  our children  thousands journeyed from Southern  provinces for a two-day National strike  [Applause]  Jessica Mendoza traveled from Juan  cabelica an Andean region in central  Peru I'm Marching for my children and  grandchildren Dina leave we don't want  to see you anymore you're stubborn and  you must leave  in the south of the country hundreds  tried to take over the airports in July  the president addressed the nation on  Thursday evening she called for dialogue  but vowed to prosecute protesters who  had broken the law quien Los financia a  new protest is no social agenda you are  outside the law you want to break the  rule of law and generate chaos and  disorder you want to take power  more than 50 people have died in  protests since former president Pedro  Castillo was ousted and arrested six  weeks ago  that's the question for many peruvians  most disapprove of baluata's governments  and believe she should start considering  stepping down  [Music]  opinion polls say 71 of peruvians don't  agree with boluarte and half of the  country support the protesters demands  the state of emergency in the country  has been extended nearly 100 roads have  been blocked the economic losses are  enormous and there appears to be no end  in sight to the spiraling unrest Marina  Sanchez al-jacida Lima  [Music]

